ar":2011,"disposition":"sustained","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga39_2011__2009-28.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga39_2011__2009-28.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"wsc:Q.14--case:cases/ga39_2011__2009-28.md:occ-1f09d71ae8f8","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga39_2011__2009-28.html","repository_path":"cases/ga39_2011__2009-28.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":160},"excerpt":"…Sin is “any lack of conformity unto, or transgression of, the law of God.\" (WSC 14). Neither the Shepherding Committee nor the Presbytery provides any explanation as to how a Member's conduct could be described as “sin\", yet there be no strong presumption…","sources":["case_markdown_text"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga39_2011__2009-28.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["BCO 31-2 investigation","strong presumption of guilt","pastoral vs judicial discipline","chargeable offense","incomplete record","presbytery investigation","shepherd committee","pastoral vs. judicial responsibility","shepherding committee","adequacy of investigation","record of the case","sin vs. offense distinction"],"standard_of_review":"factual_findings","review_standards":["factual_findings"],"case_numbers":["2009-28"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"Nashville Presbytery's BCO 31-2 investigation of reports against TE George Grant was inadequately documented and internally inconsistent, and the evidence before Presbytery raised a strong presumption of guilt, the SJC held. It sustained the complaint, ruling that pastoral reconciliation does not displace the duty to institute judicial process, and remanded for further proceedings; it did not adjudicate Grant guilty. A concurrence agreed only in remanding to supplement the record under BCO 42-5 & 7."}}],